00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to be joined today by dr.  alot yom tov who is a principal  researcher at microsoft research he  studies what internet data can tell us  about health and medicine he is also the  author of crowd-sourced health how what  you do on the internet will improve  medicine uh dr. yom tov it's so great to  have you on you know we talked about  this a little bit a year or two ago in  the context of predicting or tracking  blue breakouts in the United States but  let's approach this more generally first  give us a sense of some of the different  areas in which internet search data can  be used to learn about health and  medicine so perhaps we should start by  saying what what do we mean by internet  data and roughly speaking it's what we  generate the data that we generate when  we browse the internet and this can be  things like the search queries that we  ask Google or Bing or Yahoo it can be  the things that we write on social media  on Twitter or Facebook and sometimes  it's the things that patients will write  on patient forums and things of that  nature and over the past several years  we've realized that these data are  actually a boon for medical research and  they can help in roughly speaking three  main areas the first as you mentioned is  things like the flu where many people  will use the internet to ask about the  flu but many fewer people will actually  bother visiting the doctor so we  essentially see or have data for many  more people who have the flu then  traditional medical researchers another  area is when we were on to have people  reporting things to edge with which they  find difficult to report this can be  because they're embarrassed by it or  because they just don't realize that  these are things that are related to one  another and I can give an example later  and the last is when the internet is the  primary source or the primary point at  which people interact when they want to  find some information about some medical  condition or when they go online in  order to chat to other people about  their medical  so my my first question is more of a of  a logistical question which is as a  researcher looking into this data how do  you account for example for uh adjusting  data for uh more or less internet access  for example so if you were to look at  data about could be flu but there's  probably better examples that might be  more regionally regionally oriented or  impacted how do you account when you  analyze the data for the reality that a  population that may have a higher  incidence of a particular condition or  symptom may have a higher or lower rate  of access to the Internet in the first  place just as an example of how you  would you would manipulate or evaluate  data so in some cases we know how many  people have access to the Internet and  how many of them actually use the  internet to search for that medical  condition but in other cases I mean if  you take the extreme if people don't go  online we will never see them but that  is becoming a smaller and smaller  minority both in terms of income and in  terms of age where we had problems in  the past but they're slowly disappearing  we do know how different demographics of  people access the internet and so we can  compensate for these kinds of effects  it's not always critical to do that if  you're looking at a specific population  and you just want to be able to say  something about that population in  others it's very important to be able to  say this represents a very specific  portion of the population and we know  how to adjust for that when it comes to  the flu vaccine and I this is sort of on  my mind I recently got my flu shot  earlier this week and it's one that is  often mentioned when we talked about  using search trends to to make some  assessments uh how can we distinguish or  in what ways have you research different  aspects of flu vaccination and by that I  mean on the one hand there's the  question of interest in the flu vaccine  or a portion of people that are actually  getting the vaccine and separately from  that there's the estimating of the  effectiveness of a flu  vaccine what are you looking at when you  try to estimate the effectiveness of a  flu vaccine is it searches about up or  is it particular searches or what else  is it so it's really a combination of  all of the above the first kinds of  research that used internet data to look  at the flu try to estimate how many  people actually have the flu in a given  area at a given time and Google had a  website called Google Flu Trends which  try to report this in the way that this  was done was just say let's count the  number of people asking about specific  issues related to the flu in each of  these geographic areas we have looked at  a slightly different question which  relates to the flu vaccine and that was  specifically in England where they ran a  very interesting vaccination campaign to  vaccinate children primary school  children against the flu where the  question was is this an effective way to  prevent the flu not in these children so  much but in say older populations and  younger populations which are more at  risk if they actually suffer from the  flu and they ran a very interesting  experiment where they vaccinated these  children in seven cities in England and  did not vaccinate in all the rest of  England and then they came to our  collaborators in to us and asked was  this vaccine effective and what we did  was to compare the number of people who  were asking about the flu in these Seven  Cities compared to people in other  cities of course we had to compensate  for demographics and for other factors  but the end result was that we were able  to show that indeed this vaccine was  highly effective in preventing the fluid  reduced the the number of cases by  between 25 and 30 percent give us some  some examples of sort of the exciting  advancements that may come from this  type of research and I think one side of  it is what can we sort of assess but  looking in the rearview mirror about of  incidents of something or or what we've  been talking about so  but in a more forward-looking way what  what sort of doors are open by being  able to do this type of research I think  there are two areas one is the more  personal one which is for example a few  years ago we were able to show that we  can help people with say mood disorders  know that they're going to have an  episode a manic episode up to a day  before they actually have it and that  helps them prepare for it or a few  months ago colleagues of mine showed  that by looking at the series of  searches that people make they were able  to predict that a person has specific  forms of cancer before that person knew  it now granted this is not accurate  enough at this point to be to provide a  screening tool but it shows the the way  that this may be going in the sense that  some years in the future we may be able  to tell people look the the searches  that you've asked being or Google  they're not individual symptoms that  you're experiencing they may be  connected to each other and you may want  to talk to your physician about why  you're experiencing these symptoms so  let me pause there just make sure that's  fascinating I wanted let me follow up on  that a little bit and then we'll move on  to the second part so conceivably if  there was enough data to say that you  know when people search for XY and Z in  this particular way within some span of  time of each other even if they are not  aware necessarily even that these  symptoms or searches are related we  might be able to have enough data where  those searches together could be seen as  establishing that someone is at  particularly high risk for a condition  that may not even be obvious to them  based on those searches and your browser  or whatever tool you're using might be  able to say hey you search for this two  weeks ago and this will week ago in this  today you should talk to a doctor about  whatever it is that that they might be  at risk for is that the idea yeah so so  with some forms of cancer let's say  ovarian cancer the symptoms are benign  and each of the symptoms by itself does  not cause people to worry or does not  them too worried enough to go and see a  physician and so you need something like  a program or a search engine that runs  in the background and says and collects  these symptoms to say you know the the  bloating of today and the say the the  fullness that you asked about yesterday  they're not independent of each other  and there is somehow related and you  should see a doctor and the problem with  something like ovarian cancer is that a  lot of times by the time women get to  their physician it's very late into the  disease where because of this problem  that each of the symptoms was not  worrying enough if we can somehow look  at all these symptoms together and say  you know there's a pattern here and you  should be you take this into account  then we may be able to help these people  to nudge them in the right direction  fascinating and then the second element  of this that you were getting to the  second element it relates to population  health and there are questions that  epidemiologists and and medical  practitioners want to ask about entire  populations and it's very expensive or  even sometimes impossible to answer them  let me give you an example a few years  ago there was a law passed in Israel  that forbade showing models fashion  models with a brain mass with a body  mass index that was very low and the  basic idea was that these very thin  models somehow influence people to  develop an eating disorder now that's a  question that's really hard to measure  in the physical world how do you know  that the billboard you see when you  drive to work or those ads in the  newspaper where you see a very thin  model cause somebody to develop an  eating disorder if you wanted to measure  this in the real world you'd have to go  and collect a very large sample of say  you know thousands or tens of thousands  of people and see what they see and then  analyze those data for us it was a much  simpler exercise because we  see what people look for online and we  can ask are they browsing these very  thin celebrities do they see their  images and then later on do they develop  an eating disorder or is there in some  indicator that they have an eating  disorder and so answering these very  difficult questions about populations is  much easier in certain cases with  internet data well the work is  absolutely fascinating we've been  speaking with dr. alladi amitabh who is  a principal researcher at Microsoft  Research he also is the author of  crowd-sourced health how what you do on  the internet will improve medicine uh  thank you so much for talking to us  about this today thank you very much  David

